"How did you configure Prometheus and Grafana on EKS?"

Say:

"I deployed Prometheus and Grafana on an AWS EKS cluster using the kube-prometheus-stack Helm chart. First, I created the EKS cluster and a dedicated
monitoring namespace. Then I added the Prometheus community Helm repository and installed the kube-prometheus-stack chart, which deployed Prometheus, Grafana, 
AlertManager, Node Exporter, and kube-state-metrics. After deployment, I verified all pods and exposed Grafana using a Kubernetes LoadBalancer service. 
I accessed Grafana using the LoadBalancer endpoint and used the preconfigured Kubernetes dashboards to monitor cluster, node, pod, and application metrics."




Interview Answer: How did you configure Prometheus and Grafana on Kubernetes (GKE)?

"I configured Prometheus and Grafana on a GKE cluster using Helm and the kube-prometheus-stack chart."

Step 1: Create the Kubernetes Cluster
First, I created a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster.
Then I connected to the cluster using kubectl.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 2: Create a Dedicated Namespace
I created a separate namespace named monitor.
This helps isolate monitoring resources from application workloads and makes management easier.
command:- kubectl create namespace monitor
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 3: Add the Helm Repository
I added the Prometheus Community Helm repository and updated it to get the latest charts.
command:- helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts

command:- helm repo update
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 4: Install Prometheus and Grafana
I installed the kube-prometheus-stack Helm chart.

This chart automatically deploys:
Prometheus Server
Grafana
Alertmanager
Node Exporter
kube-state-metrics
Prometheus Operator

command:- helm install kube-prometheus-stack prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack \--namespace monitor
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 5: Verify the Installation
After deployment, I verified that all monitoring components were running successfully.

command:- kubectl get pods -n monitor
I ensured all pods were in the Running state.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 6: Expose Grafana
Since Grafana needs to be accessed from a browser, I exposed the Grafana deployment using a LoadBalancer Service.
kubectl expose deployment kube-prometheus-stack-grafana \
--port=3000 \
--target-port=3000 \
--name=grafana \
--type=LoadBalancer \
-n monitor
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 7: Verify the Service
I verified that the LoadBalancer service was created successfully.
command:- kubectl get svc -n monitor
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Then I copied the External IP and accessed Grafana using:
http://<External-IP>:3000
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 8: Login to Grafana
I used the default credentials:
Username: admin
Password: Retrieved from the Kubernetes Secret.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 9: Explore Dashboards
After logging in, I verified the preconfigured dashboards.
The dashboards automatically displayed:
Cluster Health
Node CPU Usage
Memory Usage
Disk Utilization
Pod Metrics
Namespace Metrics
Kubernetes Resource Utilization





